  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none; the full paper text is present.",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The study measures cross-model similarity of SAE features and semantic subspaces, not the share of neuron poles with one or more jobs, explanations tested on held-out activations, or neuron-based attribution graphs.",
    "gate_note": "The reported results concern SAE-derived features and their cross-model alignment, a related but different construct from the claim's neuron-pole job shares and explanation evaluations. The study does not report measurements of those claim versions. Rows below record its related feature-alignment results; they are linked to none of the versions.",
    "test_reason": "The study compares pretrained SAE features across Gemma-2-2B and Gemma-2-9B using SVCCA and RSA. It never measures what share of raw neurons or neuron poles have one job, never scores neuron explanations, and builds no neuron-based attribution graphs. Its only mention of polysemantic neurons is background that motivates using SAEs. It neither tests nor uses the claim's finding, so it is T6. 'Discusses' is kept as the relation because the related-work section mentions polysemantic neurons and superposition.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. The authors conclude that Gemma-2-2B and 9B share aligned SAE feature spaces, most strongly in middle layers, and that coherent concept pairs and a multi-token subspace align. They note the study covers only two models with the same tokenizer and few subspaces.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study aligns SAE-derived features across model layers and measures representational similarity; it does not audit neuron-pole job descriptions or test natural-language neuron explanations on held-out activations.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The reported alignment varies by layer. 1-to-1 matching yields somewhat higher scores than many-to-1 matching. The authors report no statistically significant change from using cosine similarity or Euclidean distance instead of Pearson correlation.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Paired SVCCA between SAE feature spaces of Gemma-2-2B and Gemma-2-9B is strongest in middle layers. It peaks at 0.73 with 1-to-1 matching, against random-pair baselines of about 0.005–0.034. It is weaker at early layers and the last layer.",
        "proposed_claim": "SAE feature spaces of differently sized models in the same family align most strongly in middle layers.",
